# KNOWN_ISSUES.md

> Found, not yet worked. Every issue is recorded here **the moment it is
> found**, whoever found it: an agent, CI, a hosted reviewer, a scanner, or
> the owner. Referenced by `CLAUDE.md`. Reading it comes before choosing a
> CI path.

**Three records, three tenses** (`docs/WALL_STANDARDS.md` section 4). The
wall shows what is **in flight**. This file holds what is **known**.
`FAILURE_PATTERNS.md` holds what is **fixed and guarded**. A finding parked
in the wrong tense is invisible to the reader who needed it.

## The discipline, in order

1. **Record it on arrival.** Before reproducing, before fixing, before
   replying. A finding that exists only in a review thread disappears when the
   pull request merges.
2. **Group it into a family and name the shared root.** Group by mechanism,
   not by symptom. Three variations of one root cost three cycles when they
   could have cost one.
3. **Elevate at three.** A family with three members is a candidate
   recurring class. It gets an entry in `FAILURE_PATTERNS.md` with a
   `> class-guard:` line, a mutation proving the guard can fail, and a
   `VARIANT:` fixture.
4. **Choose the CI path from this file.** Nothing goes to a mutation run, a
   reviewer, a push or a CI run until this file has been read and the family
   named. The cheapest, most focused path plus its blast radius is a claim
   about what could be wrong, and this file is the list of what is.
5. **Leave in one of two ways.** An entry leaves when it lands in
   `FAILURE_PATTERNS.md` with a guard, or when it is declined with a reason
   and the name of whoever decided. There is no third state. An entry that
   stops being mentioned has been eliminated, whatever the intent: silence is
   not a disposition.

Three more rules keep the file honest:

- **Late entries are marked late.** An entry written after work on it began
  says so; the lateness is itself a recordable lapse. Needing a periodic sweep
  of old review threads is proof that step 1 was skipped.
- **Elevation can be declined, but only in writing.** Give the reason (for
  example, an existing class already enforces the root, so the member is
  attached there) and the condition under which the NEXT member would
  elevate.
- **Every open entry has an owner and a checkable "closes when".** An entry
  nobody owns and nobody can check is a wish.

An entry with a code shape may carry a fenced `ast-grep` block and an
`ast-grep-test` block. It becomes a report-only structural rule on the next
commit (`docs/SCAN_LANE.md`), so the rest of the tree is searched for the same
shape while the fix is still being written.

## Entry format

```
## KI-<YYYY-MM-DD>-<letter> - <one line: what is wrong, as a mechanism>
- Found by      who or what raised it, and where
- Symptom       what it looks like from outside
- Family        the shared root (a family below, or a new one)
- Blast radius  what it touches if left
- Owner         who moves it next
- Closes when   the checkable condition that ends it
- Next step     the cheapest action that moves it to guarded or declined
- Disposition   open | guarded -> F-<SCOPE>-<NNN> | declined (see below)
```

