## Families - the shared root, not the symptom

A family is named in one sentence that predicts where the next member will
appear, never by the surface it showed up on ("a YAML bug"). These thirteen
were genericized from three production deployments (a resident app, an
appliance, a research repo) and are lettered by how often the mechanism
recurred across them.

<!-- Lettered by how often the mechanism recurred across the three source
deployments, most-recurring first. Keep the ones that can happen to you. -->

### A - A check passes for a reason other than the one it names

The thing that went green (or red) was not the thing under test. The check
reads an artifact near to hand instead of the one that can fail: the file on
disk instead of the program that ran, the copy one consumer reads instead of
the copy the claim is about, a count of badges instead of the rendered fields,
a metric whose terms make it a constant. Its error path often returns the same
value as its clean path, so "could not run" reads as "found nothing". It
recurs inside the guards written against it.

How it shows up:
- The assertion still passes when the property it claims is deleted, or a
  mutation is "caught" by an unrelated, cheaper clause.
- A guard that has never been observed failing, or whose live corpus is empty
  so its clause is vacuously true.
- A gauge or verdict that reads the same on an idle and a saturated system,
  or an `A or B` where A can never be false.
- A reviewer or scanner reports "clean" on a diff it excluded or declined.
- A checker's error branch drops the unreadable input and answers "no
  problems".

What elevates it: any guard without a revert-probe, or a second instance in
one subsystem. The class-guard records which clause is expected to go red for
each mutation and requires that clause among the failures, and asserts both
the low case reads low and the high case still reads high.

Seen in: resident app (3), appliance (2), research repo (2)

### B - A verdict reached before the read that would falsify it

A conclusion about a cause, a constraint or a cost is formed from memory or
from polling the nearest machinery, instead of from the record that would
disprove it: the object's own state field, a diff of two runs, the measured
durations, the way sibling repositories already met the same vendor. The
verdict then drives action (a "not ours" note, a workaround, a cut) that
treats the wrong thing.

How it shows up:
- The same query repeated with an unchanged result, then a claim that "the
  system is not doing X".
- "Flaky" or "the provider's fault" when the same step fails with the same
  error on fresh infrastructure over unchanged inputs.
- A cost cut aimed at last month's expensive step while the spend is in run
  count or in lanes that start only to say they are paused.
- A set of options presented as complete for an external limit that a sibling
  project has already removed.

What elevates it: a claim about someone else's infrastructure, or a
cost-motivated removal, about to leave the session. Name the falsifying query
first (object fields, run diff, measured durations, sibling grep) and run it
before the claim or the change goes out.

Seen in: appliance (2), resident app (1), research repo (1)

### C - A fix or guard covers the instances in hand, not the category

A fix's population is the set of cases that produced it; the category's
population is every place that does that kind of thing. A canonical helper
migrates the two call sites that prompted it; a parser is fixtured from the
local corpus and never meets the format's other freedoms; a detector is
seeded with the known instances and finds only what the register already
knows; three hand-written lists agree with each other and are jointly wrong.

How it shows up:
- "Fixed in X", then weeks later "same defect in Y" one directory away.
- Valid input of an unseen style parses as empty, or the corrected parser
  rejects a neighbouring valid style.
- A new category, file or page is covered by nothing and the guard stays
  green.
- A membership check run in one direction over two collections that must
  agree.

What elevates it: any recurrence. The fix must ship with a mechanical sweep
(grep or AST walk) of every site, a guard stated over the category ("nobody
does this by hand", with a written-reason escape), and fixtures that vary the
format's freedoms rather than copy its content.

Seen in: research repo (3), resident app (1)

### D - The rule is written down, but nothing makes it fire

The correct rule already exists: in a style guide, a docstring, a sibling
job, a comment warning about the trap, or the failure registry as prose. It
recurs anyway because it relies on someone re-noticing it at authoring time,
or its trigger fires only at session start. The registry itself degrades the
same way when entries are added without searching it first.

How it shows up:
- "The tree already knew, in another file"; a comment documents a trap and
  leaves it in place.
- The same category appears in two consecutive findings roll-ups; a finding
  cites a rule the team already has.
- A registry entry marked "enforced by discipline" or "no mechanical gate
  yet" recurs within days.
- Two ids, or two contradicting remedies, for one mechanism.

What elevates it: recurrence after the rule was written, or the rule being
written a third time. It becomes a lint, a structural rule, a shared helper,
or (for shapes with no code form) a checklist question answered in the pull
request body. Registry additions get a duplicate-id test and a
search-before-filing step.

Seen in: resident app (4)

### E - The box that runs it is not the box CI tested

CI runs on a fresh install of one operating system; the code runs on a
long-lived machine that differs in OS behaviour (encoding, file locks, shell
parsing, event-loop types, service identities), in accumulated state (stale
dependency directories, mixed-version packages, transitive dependencies that
floated) and in third parties CI never meets (endpoint security reading a
command line as malware).

How it shows up:
- "Passed CI, broke on the user's box" with an OS-specific error.
- A fix verified in the author's shell that fails under a scheduler or
  service account.
- A failure that survives re-running setup, because setup is idempotent over
  the broken state.
- A launcher blocked by endpoint security because locally sound flags
  compose into an attack signature.

What elevates it: the second box-only failure in the same mechanism. Add a
structural test or a lane for that platform, and post-install probes that
exercise the failing symbol on the real box.

Seen in: resident app (2), appliance (1)

### F - The fix reproduces the defect one level down

A fix written against class X ships containing class X, or an instance of
another known class, because it is read only against the class it fixes. A
preflight built to surface a discarded diagnostic discards it; a timeout
added to stop hangs is sized from an unmeasured number; a presence check
lands inside a value fix.

How it shows up:
- The defect is found in review of the fix, not in the original change.
- The fix's own tests pass because they share the fix's assumption.
- Review round N finds a defect in the guard added in round N-1.

What elevates it: run the class's own check against the fix diff before
merge, and read every new guard against the whole class list ("which other
class is this guard an instance of?"). It becomes mechanical when every new
guard is run through the registry's class-guards.

Seen in: resident app (1), research repo (1)

### G - Fails once, passes on retry

A check that fails on first run and passes on retry is read as flaky or over-
strict, and the first instinct is to widen a timeout, raise a limit or loosen
the gate. The retry passed because the first attempt warmed something, or
because the race did not land that time: a cache whose first caller pays a
cost nobody else pays, shared state leaking across tests, ordering assumed
from random values.

How it shows up:
- Green on one CI event type, red on another, same commit.
- A timeout or memory failure with zero assertion failures.
- A test that passes alone and fails in the suite under random ordering.
- A timeout bump that reduces the flake but does not eliminate it.

What elevates it: any proposal to loosen a gate or add a retry, or a flake
that survives one increase. Find the stateful thing underneath, run the suite
in random order locally, and add a deterministic synchronisation test.

Seen in: resident app (1), appliance (1)

### H - A capability goes dark on changes to itself

Anything with a self-referential precondition goes quiet exactly where it is
most needed: a hosted reviewer refuses to run on a change to the workflow
that invokes it and still reports success; a guard that other probes select
fails whenever they mutate its inputs, so neither can be trusted.

How it shows up:
- A green check with near-zero duration on exactly the changes touching the
  check's own configuration.
- A guard whose failures coincide with unrelated tooling runs.

What elevates it: a second occurrence. Decide the precondition before
invoking the capability and report BLOCKED in words; a precheck that cannot
decide must not silently remove the capability.

Seen in: appliance (1), resident app (1, recorded as a deliberate non-fix)

### I - The machinery is built and the last inch is not wired

A change builds the right parts and stops one step short of making them
count: an event constant registered and never published, a counter defined
and never incremented, a call recorded by a fake and never asserted, a failure
caught and turned into a silent default. Each reads as done in the diff.

How it shows up:
- New constants, counters or captures with no use site in the same diff.
- A test double records calls and no assertion reads the recording.
- An exception handler returning a safe default with no counter, log or
  surfaced status.

What elevates it: a second recurrence after an explicit rule. It becomes a
diff check: every added identifier of kind event, counter or capture has a
use site in the same diff.

Seen in: resident app (1)

### J - A property across instances is invisible to a per-instance check

A suite is organised by the artifact it reads, so a requirement that ranges
over two artifacts has nowhere to live. Every instance is well formed and
every clause passes; the defect is in the relation: duplicate subjects across
records, one label rendered from two fields, a producer scheduled after its
consumer, two report sections that contradict each other.

How it shows up:
- A symptom the owner can describe that no single-run check can move.
- Requirements containing "same", "unique among", "agrees with", "before".
- A report printing two statements that cannot both be true.

What elevates it: a third artifact PAIR. The guard loads both artifacts and
asserts the relation.

Seen in: research repo (1)

### K - A writer assumes a shared namespace is empty

A mechanism that writes into indexed slots or registrations it does not own
assumes it is the only writer: it starts at slot 0 of a numbered key family
the host already uses, or fails forever on a stale registration that outlived
its directory.

How it shows up:
- Setting the first slot of a numbered configuration family.
- "Add" commands that fail permanently on leftover state.

What elevates it: a third instance. Read the namespace first, append at the
next free index, and treat an unreadable count as "do not write", never as
"start at zero".

Seen in: appliance (1)

### L - The error path does not render where the success path does

A component that relocates itself to render (an overlay, a portal) keeps an
error boundary that renders in place, so its failure draws inside a clipped
container: present, invisible. Without any boundary, one malformed field
blanks the whole page and hides the cause in a console nobody reads.

How it shows up:
- A control that "does nothing" on a path that threw during render.
- A whole-app crash from one unvalidated nested field.

What elevates it: a second relocated component. Put the escape mechanism
outside the thing that can fail, and make every boundary name the failing
panel.

Seen in: appliance (1)

### M - Review capacity treated as unlimited

Metered or rate-limited hosted reviewers turn process habits into money and
waiting time: every incremental push spends a review, a push mid-review
wastes the one in flight, wide changes need several rounds, and "unlimited"
plans still throttle at a usage cap. The limit is hit mid-change, exactly
when the review is needed.

How it shows up:
- Review rounds per merged change well above one; rate-limit notices during
  ordinary iteration.
- Titles or paths meant to skip review still consuming reviews.
- A per-change review cost that nothing reports.

What elevates it: measuring spend per change against a target (for example
at most one hosted round per change); after that, a rate-limit hit is a
tracked incident. Prevention is demand-side: a local review pass first,
batched pushes, narrow changes.

Seen in: resident app (1)

