## Deliberate non-fixes worth copying

Decisions NOT to fix, each with its reason and what would reopen it. When one
of these applies here, record it in the Declined table below with who decided,
so a later session does not re-find it and argue it again.


- **A check that refuses to run on a change to its own configuration is left
  refusing**, because working around it would let a change rewrite its own
  reviewer's permissions; the skip is made visible and the change is
  validated by the next one after merge. Reopen only if the platform offers a
  trusted way to validate such changes.
- **A safety gate halts when its dependency is down, and authoritative alarms
  are never debounced**, because degrading quietly or swallowing a real alarm
  is worse than a stop or a page; only flap-prone readings are held, and a
  held reading renders as indeterminate, never green. Reopen if the halts
  themselves become the dominant outage.
- **Never auto-reclaim something that may still be live** (a lock, a
  process, shared lifecycle state); cleanup degrades toward KEEP when
  evidence is unclear, because wrongly keeping self-heals through staleness
  while wrongly releasing kills live work. Reopen when liveness can be proven,
  not inferred.
- **When unsure, err in the direction whose mistake is visible and cheap**:
  over-accept references in a dead-code rule, under-claim "done", keep items
  too short to grade, fail open to running CI when mergeability is unknown,
  because a rule that cries wolf gets ignored and a fabricated result is worse
  than a missing one. Reopen if the lenient direction is shown to hide a real
  defect.
- **No tree-wide gate for a shape that is too common**; baseline it with a
  ratchet and enforce the rest in review, because a gate that drowns in false
  positives gets switched off. Reopen when a precise structural rule exists
  for the shape.
- **Mutation testing is required only for a test just written to pin one
  behaviour**, not tree-wide, because that is where false confidence is
  highest and the run is cheap. Reopen if surviving mutants keep appearing in
  older tests.
- **Do not ship a guard or test that cannot fail**; record the rule with an
  owner and a closing condition and make the mistake loud another way (a
  lock, a log), because an inert probe reads as coverage. Reopen when a fault
  injection can make the guard go red.
- **Decline with evidence, not silence**: a reviewer finding judged wrong is
  answered with the reasoning and a falsifying condition, and a disproved
  leading claim is recorded separately from any real residual hazard, because
  a silent decline is re-found and re-argued. Reopen when the falsifying
  condition is observed.
- **Do not discount a review lane wholesale because it fabricates in one
  shape**; refute that shape mechanically and keep the lane, because the same
  lane finds defects nobody else does. Reopen if its refuted shapes outnumber
  its real findings.
- **Control review cost demand-side, not by buying throughput or dropping
  confirmations**: keep per-commit re-review where the confirmation is
  load-bearing, and do not enable usage-based overage, because paid headroom
  hides the process defect. Reopen if narrow, batched changes still hit the
  limit.
- **A fix outside the change's scope goes in its own change, with the
  residual disclosed**, because bolting a store-level primitive, a design
  collision or a deployment decision onto an in-flight change late widens its
  blast radius; the interim state is loud, never reported as success. Reopen
  as its own planned change.
- **Do not guess a contract you cannot verify**: record the mismatch visibly
  on every run, keep one working source rather than building on an
  unverifiable vendor mechanism, and do not narrow a permission list on a
  guess, because a guess replaces a visible failure with an invisible wrong
  answer. Reopen when the real contract or the refused command is in hand.
- **Decline broad transforms and coercion layers at a boundary** (a
  heuristic with no shape rule, a catch-all normaliser), pinning current
  behaviour with a test and repairing at the source data, because they turn
  loud bugs into quiet wrong output. Reopen when a transform beats every
  pinned known case.
- **Withdraw a change whose review rounds cannot be verified locally and
  whose every fix enlarges the surface**, because each round is new
  unexercised code; reintroduce it later in a shape the found defects cannot
  take. Reopen when the path can be exercised locally.
- **Derived files are strict where the tree is clean and lenient where it is
  legitimately rewritten**: strict on the clean main CI run, tolerated on
  local checkouts and branches, never widened to mirror untracked files, and
  display-only outputs kept out of the integrity manifest, because otherwise
  the derived file fights merges and local tooling. Reopen if runtime-affecting
  content ever leaves the strict set.
- **A gate does not fetch from someone else's system**; the recorded surface
  is regenerated by hand and the check runs offline, because a gate that
  fetches goes red when another site is down. Reopen if the recorded surface
  drifts unnoticed.
- **State a platform limit rather than "fix" it, and carry the caveat with
  the data** (client-side hooks where server-side enforcement is unavailable,
  unverifiable TLS on a device with a self-signed certificate, what static
  hosting cannot prevent), because a control that breaks a working
  configuration gets switched off and then protects nothing. Reopen when the
  platform offers the real control.
- **A known-unfixable flake is documented so it is recognised, not chased**:
  reduce parallelism or skip with a registry tag plus a sentinel test pinning
  the wiring, because no assertion-level guard exists for it. Reopen when the
  runtime or test library moves.
- **Decline a fix whose context defeats it** (a lock around a call that may
  never return, an in-process counter in a process that exits every tick),
  because it reinstates the failure or measures nothing; report the true state
  and name the falsifying condition. Reopen if that condition is observed.
- **Decline elevating a family whose root an existing class already
  enforces**; attach the member's guard, mutation and VARIANT to that class,
  because a second copy of a rule drifts from the first. Reopen when a member
  arrives that the existing class cannot cover.
- **When the property is syntactic, assert it on the source**: accept an
  equivalent mutant as survived, and accept a pinned number only when no
  second source exists (and say so), because a behavioural test cannot see a
  syntactic rule. Reopen if a behavioural seam appears.

